  'One of three Worcester branches paid for by a Carnegie grant of $75,000 in 1910. Andrew and Louise Carnegie were there when its cornerstone was laid on March 26, 1913, the last of their three stops that day; it opened in February 1914. Lucius W. Briggs designed it, Classical with a little Spanish Mission: one storey under a tile hip roof, Corinthian pilasters and a half-round pediment over the door. Renamed in 1994 for Frances Perkins, the first woman in a United States cabinet; enlarged in 1996.',
