N('athol-carnegie-library','Athol Public Library (Carnegie building)','Athol','cmass','civic','standing',42.59414,-72.22851,'exact','open','walk','','1916–1918',
  'The dates differ: Wikipedia\'s list gives a grant of $22,000 in 1902; New England Carnegies says the town turned down $15,000 early in the century and took $22,000 in 1916, and the building was handed to the town on August 16, 1918. Designed by W. H. and Henry McLean of Boston in the Classical style. A rear addition came in 1965, air conditioning in 1969 (the first in Athol), and a large addition in 2015 that New England Carnegies calls the only LEED Platinum Carnegie enlargement.',
  'Tan brick with pale stone trim, as its picture shows.',
  '','568 Main Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Athol Public Library','https://www.athollibrary.org/about-us'],NEC('maathol','Athol'),W('Athol, Massachusetts'),LIST],
  brick='no',conf='mixed',hours='Mon, Wed and Thu 9:30 a.m.–6 p.m.; Tue 9:30–8; Fri 9:30–1:30; closed Saturday, Sunday and holidays.'),
N('berkley-carnegie-library','Berkley Public Library (Carnegie building)','Berkley','south','civic','standing',41.84539,-71.08286,'exact','open','drive','','1915–1919',
  'A Carnegie grant of $5,000 (1915 in Wikipedia\'s list, 1918 in New England Carnegies); the library opened without fanfare on Saturday, December 12, 1919, because of the war, and the Soldiers Monument Association finished the basement. The architect is not known. New England Carnegies says it has changed very little inside or out.',
  'Red brick with pale trim under a hip roof, as its picture shows.',
  '','1 North Main Street (the town\'s page says 2 North Main Street). The library\'s own site could not be read, so its hours and parking were not checked.',
  [NEC('maberkley','Berkley'),['Town of Berkley: Berkley Public Library','https://townofberkleyma.gov/venue/berkley-public-library'],LIST],
  brick='yes',conf='mixed',hours='?'),
N('brockton-carnegie-library','Brockton Public Library (Carnegie main library)','Brockton','south','civic','standing',42.07941,-71.01929,'exact','open','drive','','1910–1913',
  'Mayor Clifford wrote to Carnegie in 1910; Carnegie agreed to $75,000 and raised it to $110,000, and the city promised $11,000 a year. Dedicated on June 10, 1913, it was designed by Nathaniel C. Smith of New Bedford in the Beaux Arts style, with white marble and quartered oak inside. Fritz Fuglister painted WPA murals round the rotunda in 1941; the skylights have since been covered. An addition of 2003 more than doubled it. It is now the Thomas P. Kennedy Main Library.',
  'Brick and limestone: dark red-brown brick over a pale stone ground floor, as its picture shows.',
  '','304 Main Street. The library says there is plenty of off-street parking.',
  [['Brockton Public Library: history','https://www.brocktonpubliclibrary.org/182/History-of-the-Library'],['Brockton Public Library: hours and visiting','https://www.brocktonpubliclibrary.org/185/Hours-Visiting'],NEC('mabrockton','Brockton'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Mon and Tue noon–9 p.m.; Wed 9–9; Thu–Sat 9–5; Sunday not listed.'),
N('chelsea-carnegie-library','Chelsea Public Library (Carnegie building)','Chelsea','bos','civic','standing',42.39360,-71.03201,'exact','open','walk','','1908–1910',
  'The Fitz Public Library of 1885 on this corner burned in Chelsea\'s Great Fire of April 1908 with about 80,000 books. Carnegie gave $60,000 that July (Wikipedia\'s list; New England Carnegies says $57,500 with $20,000 of insurance money), and the new library went up in 1909 and was dedicated in 1910, as the plaques on it say. The land had been given in 1884 by Eustace C. Fitz. The architect was not found.',
  'A Beaux Arts front with a columned entrance, pale grey in its picture, at Broadway and Library Street.',
  '','569 Broadway, at Library Street. Parking not given on the city\'s page.',
  [['City of Chelsea: library','https://www.chelseama.gov/departments/library/'],['HMdb: Chelsea Public Library','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=198171'],NEC('machelsea','Chelsea'),LIST],
  brick='no',hours='Mon–Thu 9 a.m.–8 p.m.; Fri and Sat 9–5; closed Sunday.'),
N('bigelow-library-clinton-carnegie','Bigelow Free Public Library (Carnegie building)','Clinton','worc','civic','standing',42.41743,-71.68334,'exact','open','walk','','1901–1903',
  'A grant of $25,000 in 1901; opened in 1902 and dedicated in 1903, and the library says it is little changed. The arched ceiling and stair of the main floor were built by the Guastavino method; a frieze copied from the west front of the Parthenon was given by Henry F. Bigelow; the three-storey book stack has glass floors. A lift came in 1991. A plan for a new or rebuilt library failed at town meeting and the town election in 2026. New England Carnegies calls the style Tudor Revival, the library Greek Revival.',
  'Red brick with pale trim, as its picture shows, at Walnut and Church Streets.',
  '','54 Walnut Street, at Church Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Bigelow Free Public Library: history','https://bigelowlibrary.org/library-history/'],['Bigelow Free Public Library: hours','https://bigelowlibrary.org/hours-location/'],['Bigelow Free Public Library: the building project','https://bigelowlibrary.org/status-of-project/'],NEC('maclinton','Clinton'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Tue–Thu 9 a.m.–8 p.m.; Fri 9–6; Sat 9–5; closed Sunday and Monday.'),
N('dighton-carnegie-library','Old Dighton Public Library (Carnegie building)','Dighton','south','civic','standing',41.81543,-71.12727,'exact','view','drive','','1905–2021',
  'A grant of $6,000 in 1905 built a library of about 3,000 square feet in 1910, in the Prairie style; the architect is not known. It closed in the autumn of 2021 for building-code problems, with no safe emergency way out, and the library opened in Smith Memorial Hall (1889) at 207 Main Street in September 2025. What becomes of the old building was not found.',
  'Rounded fieldstone walls under a deep-eaved roof, as its picture shows.',
  '','395 Main Street. Closed; look from the street.',
  [['Dighton Public Library: the new library','https://dightonlibrary.org/information-new-dighton-public-library/'],['Taunton Daily Gazette, 2022','https://www.yahoo.com/news/dighton-says-big-yes-moving-080020882.html'],NEC('madighton','Dighton'),LIST],
  brick='no'),
N('edgartown-carnegie-library','The Carnegie (old Edgartown library)','Edgartown','south','civic','standing',41.39052,-70.51130,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1904',
  'A grant of $4,000 (1902 in Wikipedia\'s list, 1903 in New England Carnegies) built Edgartown\'s library in 1904, on land given by Caroline Osborn Warren; the island builder William King put it up, and no architect is named. The arched front windows carry a rising sun in yellow glass. Added to in the 1930s and later. The library left in March 2016 for a new building by the Edgartown School, and the Vineyard Preservation Trust bought and restored the old one as a heritage centre with a reading library, a shop and exhibits.',
  'Red brick with white trim, as its picture shows.',
  '','58 North Water Street. Run by the Vineyard Preservation Trust; the exhibition is $5 for adults, and private tours are by appointment. Street parking.',
  [['Vineyard Preservation Trust: the Carnegie','https://vineyardtrust.org/property/the-carnegie/'],['Vineyard Gazette, March 5, 2016','https://vineyardgazette.com/towns/edgartown/2016/03/05/story-ends-after-112-years-edgartown-carnegie-library/'],NEC('maedgartown','Edgartown'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Wed–Sat 10 a.m.–3 p.m.; closed Sunday to Tuesday.'),
N('granby-carnegie-library','Old Granby Library (Carnegie building)','Granby','pv','civic','standing',42.25509,-72.51731,'site','view','drive','','1916–1917',
  'A Carnegie grant of $5,000 in 1916 built the library in 1917, in the Colonial Revival style; the architect is not known. A new library of 12,062 square feet at 297 East State Street replaced it, and the old building holds some town offices (Daily Hampshire Gazette, 2025 and 2026) until a new town office building is finished. What becomes of it then was not found.',
  'A small white-painted wooden building with a pediment on two Ionic columns, as its picture shows.',
  '','On Library Lane. Town offices; look from the street.',
  [['STV: Granby Free Public Library','https://stvinc.com/project/town-of-granby-free-public-library/'],['Daily Hampshire Gazette, April 30, 2026','https://gazettenet.com/2026/04/30/granby-breaks-ground-on-new-town-office-building-at-old-west-street-school/'],NEC('magranby','Granby'),LIST],
  brick='no'),
N('holliston-carnegie-library','Holliston Public Library (Carnegie building)','Holliston','bos','civic','standing',42.20040,-71.42912,'exact','open','walk','','1903–1904',
  'Elizabeth S. Burnap left the site in 1900, and the Rev. Nicholas Vander Pyl wrote to Carnegie in 1903; the library cost just under $10,000 and opened on July 5, 1904, a day late because of a thunderstorm. J. Lawrence Berry of Boston designed it, and a time capsule lies under one of its pillars. The basement became the children\'s room in 1963, and an addition of 1987 about tripled it. It stands in the Thomas Hollis Historic District.',
  'Buff masonry, as its picture shows.',
  '','752 Washington Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Holliston Public Library: history','https://hollistonlibrary.org/hpl-history/'],W('Thomas Hollis Historic District'),NEC('maholliston','Holliston'),LIST],
  brick='no',hours='Mon–Wed 9:30 a.m.–8 p.m.; Thu and Fri 9:30–5; Sat 9:30–2; closed Sunday.'),
N('hudson-carnegie-library-ma','Hudson Public Library (Carnegie building)','Hudson','bos','civic','standing',42.39159,-71.57069,'exact','open','walk','','1904–1905',
  'The librarian, Grace Whittemore, asked Carnegie for help; he gave $12,500, and the library opened in November 1905, designed by Albert Randolph Ross of New York. A second storey was added in 1929 (the town) or 1932 (New England Carnegies) and an addition behind in 1966. In 2026 the town was planning a rebuilding of about $20 to $24 million: the 1966 addition stands on an unsound foundation, and the town calls the Carnegie part a landmark.',
  'A library of one storey grown to two, at Wood Square.',
  '','3 Washington Street, at Wood Square. Free two-hour street parking in front, with accessible spaces, and a two-hour public lot behind the fire station.',
  [['Town of Hudson: visit the library','https://townofhudson.org/237/Visit-Us'],['Town of Hudson: library building project','https://townofhudson.org/899/Hudson-Public-Library-Building-Project'],NEC('mahudson','Hudson'),LIST],
  brick='?',hours='Mon–Thu 9 a.m.–8:30 p.m.; Fri 9–6; Sat 9–5; closed Sunday.'),
N('lakeville-carnegie-library','Old Lakeville Public Library (Carnegie building)','Lakeville','south','civic','standing',41.84674,-70.94898,'exact','view','drive','','1912–1914',
  'A grant of $5,000 in 1912, with $2,000 raised by the Library Sewing Circle; opened in 1914, in the Romanesque style, architect unknown. The library moved across the road in 2005. The old building was a book café from 2007 (New England Carnegies) and is now the town\'s Office of Inspectional Services, given a wheelchair ramp in December 2025.',
  'Rough-faced stone with a round-arched door, as its picture shows.',
  '','241 Main Street. Town offices; look from the street.',
  [['The Week Today, December 12, 2025','https://nemasket.theweektoday.com/article/lakeville-celebrates-four-recent-community-development-projects-bringing-new-uses-and'],['Lakeville Public Library: about','https://lakevillelibrary.org/about-the-library.html'],NEC('malakeville','Lakeville'),LIST],
  brick='no'),
N('lee-library-carnegie','Lee Library (Carnegie building)','Lee','berk','civic','standing',42.30656,-73.24962,'exact','open','walk','','1903–1907',
  'Built in 1907 for $35,500: $12,000 from Carnegie, $18,300 from the town and the rest from citizens. It stands on the homestead of Peter Wilcox, where Lee\'s first town meeting met in a log house in December 1777. Its architect is Joseph McArthur Vance of Pittsfield (Lost New England) or Henry S. Moul of Hudson, New York (New England Carnegies). A wing of Lee marble in 1977 tripled it. The library says it is the only Carnegie library building left in the Berkshires.',
  'Lee marble, quarried and cut by the Lee Marble Works, with a Corinthian front and birch woodwork inside.',
  '','100 Main Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Lee Library: about','https://www.leelibraryma.org/about'],['Lost New England: public library, Lee','https://lostnewengland.com/2016/02/public-library-lee-mass/'],NEC('malee','Lee'),LIST],
  brick='no',conf='mixed',hours='Mon–Fri 10 a.m.–6 p.m.; Sat 10–2; closed Sunday.'),
N('leominster-carnegie-library','Leominster Public Library (Carnegie building)','Leominster','worc','civic','standing',42.52639,-71.76192,'exact','open','walk','','1906–1910',
  'Wikipedia\'s list gives a grant of $27,500 in 1906; the library says the town voted to take it in 1908, and the front is lettered MCMVIII. Opened in 1910, designed by Hartwell, Richardson & Driver of Boston in the Classical style. An addition of 1967 was torn down in the restoration of 2005 to 2007, which added three storeys and 34,434 square feet. A pine secretary from the library\'s beginnings in 1763 is on show.',
  'Tan brick with pale stone trim, as its picture shows, in the Monument Square Historic District.',
  '','30 West Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Leominster Public Library: hours and address','https://leominsterlibrary.org/about/hours-and-address'],['Leominster Public Library: past, present and future','https://leominsterlibrary.org/about/past-present-and-future'],NEC('maleominster','Leominster'),LIST],
  brick='no',hours='Mon–Thu 9 a.m.–9 p.m.; Fri and Sat 9–5; Sun 1–5. Shorter in summer: no Sundays, and Saturday to 1 in July and August.'),
N('lynn-haywood-branch-carnegie','Old Haywood Branch Library','Lynn','nshore','civic','standing',42.48691,-70.95968,'exact','view','walk','','1915',
  'A Beaux Arts branch library paid for by Carnegie: $50,000 in 1915 (Wikipedia\'s list) or $50,000 in 1903 for two branches (New England Carnegies). It closed in the 1990s or early 2000s after budget cuts. New England Carnegies, about 2005, found an insurance office and an estate agent in it; its use now was not checked.',
  'Buff brick, as its picture shows.',
  '','270 Broadway. Offices; look from the street.',
  [NEC('malynnhaywood','Lynn Haywood Branch'),LIST],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('lynn-houghton-branch-carnegie','Old Houghton Branch Library','Lynn','nshore','civic','standing',42.46023,-70.96880,'exact','view','walk','','1917',
  'Opened on July 19, 1917 (a Lynn Public Library photograph record), a Classical branch library from Lynn\'s Carnegie money; the architect is not known. It closed in the 1990s or early 2000s, and the city sold it to a developer for $259,000 in 2008 (Daily Item). New England Carnegies called it empty; its use now was not found.',
  'Dark red-brown brick with pale stone trim, as its picture shows.',
  '','833 Western Avenue (831 in the 2008 article). Privately owned; look from the street.',
  [['Lynn Public Library: digital heritage record','https://digitalheritage.noblenet.org/s/lynn/item/6556'],['Daily Item, May 8, 2008','https://itemlive.com/2008/05/08/former-lynn-library-sold-to-developer/'],NEC('malynnhoughton','Lynn Houghton Branch'),LIST],
  brick='yes'),
N('marlborough-carnegie-library','Marlborough Public Library (Carnegie building)','Marlborough','bos','civic','standing',42.34602,-71.55472,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1904',
  'The $30,000 grant was secured before City Hall, with the old library in it, burned on Christmas Day 1902. The land came from S. H. Howe, John A. Frye and Dennis and Sophia Witherbee, and Peabody & Stearns designed the library, opened in the autumn of 1904. An arson fire in July 1967 led to rebuilding, reopened in April 1969. In the latest project the Carnegie part was kept and restored, and the library reopened on October 2, 2023 at 37,967 square feet.',
  'The Carnegie library at the front of a larger one of 2023.',
  '','35 West Main Street. Parking for 70 cars since the 2023 enlargement.',
  [['City of Marlborough: library history','https://marlborough-ma.gov/282/History'],['City of Marlborough: library hours','https://www.marlborough-ma.gov/615/Hours'],NEC('mamarlborough','Marlborough'),LIST],
  brick='no',hours='Mon–Thu 9 a.m.–8 p.m.; Fri and Sat 9–5; Sun 1–5 (Labor Day to Memorial Day only).'),
N('melrose-carnegie-library','Melrose Public Library (Carnegie building)','Melrose','bos','civic','standing',42.45928,-71.06662,'exact','open','walk','','1904',
  'Built on the site of the old high school, which burned in 1897, and opened on April 15, 1904 (New England Carnegies gives April 20 for the dedication). Penn Varney of Lynn designed it, George M. Tufts built it, and Wilbur H. Burnham made its stained glass. An addition of 1963 was replaced by a new two-storey wing while the library worked from the old Beebe School; it reopened on April 15, 2025. On the National Register since 1988.',
  'Brick with limestone trim (Wikipedia); buff brick in its picture.',
  '','69 West Emerson Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [W('Melrose Public Library'),['Melrose Public Library: history','https://melrosepubliclibrary.org/home/mpls-history/'],NEC('mamelrose','Melrose'),LIST],
  brick='no',hours='Mon–Thu 10 a.m.–9 p.m. (upper level to 8); Fri and Sat 10–5; Sun 2–5.'),
N('millbury-carnegie-library','Millbury Public Library (Carnegie building)','Millbury','worc','civic','standing',42.19044,-71.76318,'exact','open','drive','','1915',
  'A Carnegie grant of $12,500 in 1915. Wikipedia\'s article on Millbury names William H. McLean as architect from an old report; New England Carnegies says unknown and calls the style Tudor Revival. It has been much enlarged; when was not found.',
  'Red brick with white trim, as its picture shows.',
  '','128 Elm Street. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Millbury Public Library','http://millburylibrary.org'],W('Millbury, Massachusetts'),NEC('mamillbury','Millbury'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Mon 11 a.m.–6 p.m.; Tue–Thu 10–8; Fri 10–6; Sat 9–2; closed Sunday.'),
N('needham-carnegie-library-site','Site of the Needham Carnegie Library','Needham','bos','civic','gone',42.29226,-71.23498,'area','view','drive','','1903–1960s',
  'A grant of $10,000 in 1903 built a library in Needham Heights, designed by Albert Randolph Ross; it opened on April 6, 1904 and was dedicated on June 18. Where it should stand was argued over from the start, between the Heights and the Center. In 1914 William Carter gave the land for the present library and bought the old building as an office for his mills. It was torn down in the 1960s (New England Carnegies).',
  'Nothing of it is left. What stands at the address now was not found.',
  '','968 Highland Avenue, the address New England Carnegies and Wikipedia\'s list give; not checked against any other source.',
  [NEC('maneedham','Needham'),LIST],brick='?'),
N('new-marlborough-carnegie-library-site','Site of the New Marlborough Carnegie Library','New Marlborough','berk','civic','gone',42.11302,-73.26790,'area','view','drive','','1917–1996',
  'A Carnegie grant of $5,000 in 1917 built a Colonial Revival library, finished in December 1920 and designed by William E. Kair, a local man whose parents gave the site; its columned front copied the National Mahaiwe Bank of 1847 in Great Barrington. Fire destroyed it on June 16, 1996 (a prank gone wrong, Waymarking says). The new library opened on January 1, 1999; whether it is on the same spot was not checked.',
  'The New Marlborough Town Library of 1999 stands in Mill River.',
  '','1 Mill River–Great Barrington Road, Mill River (the new library).',
  [NEC('manewmarlborough','New Marlborough'),['Waymarking: New Marlborough Town Library','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMBFNE_New_Marlborough_Town_Library_Mill_River_MA'],LIST],brick='?'),
N('reading-carnegie-library-ma','Old Reading Public Library (Carnegie building)','Reading','bos','civic','standing',42.52542,-71.10355,'exact','open','walk','','1915–1918',
  'Wikipedia\'s list gives $15,000 in 1902; New England Carnegies says $12,500 offered in 1902 went untouched and $15,000 was taken in 1915. Opened on July 3, 1918, designed by Adden & Parker of Boston in the Italian Renaissance style. The library has moved to Middlesex Avenue, and a link built in 1990 joined the old building to the Town Hall, of which it is now part.',
  'Red brick with white trim and a green roof with dormers, as its picture shows.',
  '','16 Lowell Street, as part of Reading Town Hall; the offices are open in the hours below. Parking not given.',
  [NEC('mareading','Reading'),['Reading Public Library: digital heritage record','https://digitalheritage.noblenet.org/s/reading/item/9525'],['Town of Reading','https://www.readingma.gov/'],LIST],
  brick='yes',conf='mixed',hours='Town Hall: Mon, Wed and Thu 7:30 a.m.–5:30 p.m.; Tue 7:30–7; closed Friday.'),
N('revere-carnegie-library','Revere Public Library (Carnegie building)','Revere','bos','civic','standing',42.40802,-71.00918,'exact','open','walk','','1901–1903',
  'Samantha Sparhawk of the Revere Women\'s Club and Ernest H. Pierce won a grant of $20,000, accepted on November 11, 1901; the library was built in 1902 and opened on November 18, 1903. It has never been enlarged, one of few Carnegie libraries so. Inside is hand-rubbed oak panelling; early gifts included a marble fountain, a stained-glass window and andirons. The architect was not found.',
  'Red brick with pale trim and a small dome, as its picture shows, at Beach and Library Streets.',
  '','179 Beach Street, at Library Street. Shorter hours in summer. Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [['Revere Public Library: about','https://www.reverepubliclibrary.org/about/'],['Revere Public Library: location and hours','https://www.reverepubliclibrary.org/about/location-hours/'],W('Revere Public Library'),NEC('marevere','Revere'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Mon–Thu 8 a.m.–8 p.m.; Fri and Sat 9–5; closed Sunday.'),
N('rockland-memorial-library-carnegie','Rockland Memorial Library (Carnegie building)','Rockland','south','civic','standing',42.13214,-70.91683,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1905',
  'The earlier library burned in 1890. A Carnegie grant of $12,500 (1902 in Wikipedia, 1903 in New England Carnegies) built this one, in 1903 (Wikipedia) or between 1903 and 1905 (the library), designed by McLean & Wright of Boston. On the National Register since 1989.',
  'Red brick in stretcher bond with terra cotta trim, quoins, an Ionic entrance and a dome.',
  '','20 Belmont Street, at Union Street (Wikipedia gives 382 Union Street). Parking not given on the library\'s page.',
  [W('Rockland Memorial Library'),['Rockland Memorial Library','http://rocklandmemoriallibrary.org'],NEC('marockland','Rockland'),LIST],
  brick='yes',hours='Mon–Wed 9:30 a.m.–8 p.m.; Thu–Sat 9:30–5; Sunday not listed.'),
